1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,940
This is SecureVPN: an internal web service on AWS with encrypted storage and

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:11,140
automated monitoring. It is a private document vault that you can reach only

3
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:16,250
through AWS Client VPN, and the whole stack is defined in Terraform.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:20,880
First, the security proof. With the VPN disconnected,

5
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:24,760
app.securevpn.internal does not resolve.

6
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,560
The name exists only in a Route 53 private hosted zone,

7
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:33,160
and the server has no public IP, no internet gateway and no NAT.

8
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:37,240
The hosted copy of the interface on Vercel loads, but it reports that it cannot

9
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:42,430
reach the vault service, because every API call has to travel through the VPN.

10
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:45,110
Now I connect the AWS VPN Client.

11
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,600
It authenticates with mutual TLS, using a client certificate from the project's

12
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,840
private certificate authority, and it shows a banner saying that all

13
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:55,000
connections are logged and monitored.

14
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:57,960
Only the VPC range is routed through the tunnel.

15
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,190
The same address now loads over HTTPS.

16
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:05,380
The top strip shows the four hops every request takes:

17
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:11,700
Client VPN, nginx with TLS, the vault API, and the S3 bucket,

18
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:15,410
which accepts object access only through the VPC endpoint.

19
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:20,260
I'll upload a file. It is stored in S3 with server-side encryption under the

20
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,850
project's customer-managed KMS key.

21
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:26,860
The details drawer reads the object's live S3 metadata:

22
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:31,260
the encryption type, the KMS key ARN, the S3 Bucket Key,

23
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:35,010
and the version ID. Downloads come back through the same tunnel.

24
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:39,780
When I delete a file, versioning keeps the previous versions for thirty days.

25
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:43,060
The security posture panel checks the live configuration:

26
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:47,220
bucket encryption, the Bucket Key, all four public access blocks,

27
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:52,500
versioning, TLS 1.3 on this connection, IMDSv2 on the instance,

28
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:56,450
and no public IP address. There is also a dark mode.

29
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,200
Monitoring is automated. A CloudWatch dashboard tracks the server,

30
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:04,440
the app, the VPN, and a synthetic check:

31
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,800
a Lambda inside the VPC that tests the app every five minutes.

32
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:12,320
Twelve alarms send alerts through an encrypted SNS topic.

33
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:16,960
Running make demo-alert forces the CPU alarm into the ALARM state,

34
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:19,390
and the alert email arrives in about a minute.

35
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:24,060
The same interface is also hosted on Vercel as static files.

36
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:29,060
It works only from a browser that is on the VPN and trusts the private CA.

37
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:34,140
The API allows that one origin, and it still requires a CSRF token on every

38
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,130
change.

39
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:41,000
To sum up: no internet exposure, VPN access with mutual TLS,

40
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:46,760
KMS encryption at rest, TLS in transit, and automated monitoring and alerts.

41
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:47,500
Thank you.
